TASK:{"title":"AERO9500 Final Report (Part 2) – Space Mission Design","overview":"Students design a preliminary space mission benefiting Australia, covering the space segment: mission purpose, spacecraft count, users, launch site and vehicle, orbit and orbital elements, manoeuvres, ground control and communications, mission duration and end-of-life. Design may use STK, PIGI or approved software, supplemented by hand calculations, and is reported as a concise technical report with supporting screenshots and software files."}
RUBRIC:[{"id":"C1","name":"Introduction: mission concept and rationale - Include the following required information: • What is the spacecraft's primary mission? • Why is the mission needed? • How many spacecraft are used and why? • Who are the users (operator and customers)?","weight":10,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"Addresses all requirements to a high standard. Detailed descriptions and discussions are included. References to literature as appropriate."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Addresses some requirements. Descriptions may feature lack of specific details and/or incomplete referencing."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"Many or all requirements are not addressed. Poor descriptions or lack of appropriate referencing."}]},{"id":"C2","name":"Main body: mission design - Present a summary of the mission design using software and/or hand calculations as appropriate - Include relevant screenshots and software files (in the main body of the report and/or appendix) - Include the following information: • Where it will launch from, and using which launch vehicle? Why did you choose this option? • What type of orbit will you use? What are its characteristics and orbital elements? • Will you need to perform any orbital maneuvers? • Which ground control facilities will you use? • How often will your spacecraft be able to communicate with ground control? • How long will the mission last, and what are your plans for EOL?","weight":60,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"Demonstrates high level understanding of the design procedure. Design clearly addresses mission and course concepts. Major requirements are all addressed and are well supported with quality evidence (eg. software screenshots, software files, hand calculations, references from literature). Content is comprehensive, accurate, and persuasive."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Demonstrates adequate understanding of the design procedure. Design may be inconsistent with regards to purpose and clarity of thought or feature errors. Major requirements are addressed but may lack some detail or are not well supported with evidence. Content may not be comprehensive and/or persuasive."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"Demonstrates minimal understanding of the design procedure. Major requirements are not addressed and/or lack evidence. Content is incomplete or not persuasive."}]},{"id":"C3","name":"Conclusion","weight":10,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"Detailed and appropriate conclusions reached from the evidence provided in the report."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Some conclusions are reached but they may be generic or lack specific detail."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"No actual or useful conclusions reached."}]},{"id":"C4","name":"Report presentation","weight":20,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"The document follows a clear and logical engineering report structure. The report is easy to read: well-written, with good spelling and grammar, and appropriate language style. All aspects of formatting are consistent throughout the document. Executive summary (or abstract) provides a concise overview of the report. Graphical and tabular presentation of data is appropriate, clear and consistent. References in text match reference list (and vice versa) and are cited properly."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Document is not at a professional level but does make use of headings and subheadings to indicate document structure. The report may be difficult to read: writing is just ok, broad idea comes across; spelling and grammar have some flaws, not quite appropriate language style. Although figures and tables are labelled, the formatting is unclear and/or inconsistent to the extent that the reader can lose track of the context when reading. References in text match reference list (and vice versa) and are mostly cited correctly."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"The document is poorly structured, is not coherent or shows a lack of understanding of the purpose of its sections. Much effort is required to read and understand the report: writing is poor, many mistakes with spelling and grammar, and possibly inappropriate language style (e.g. too informal). Presentation is poor to the extent that it impedes reading of the document. Examples include inconsistent formatting, and unlabelled figures or tables. References are either not cited or cited inconsistently."}]}]
MARKING:{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","met":"yes","where":"Introduction"},{"id":"R2","met":"partial","where":"Mission Design"},{"id":"R3","met":"yes","where":"Executive Summary; Introduction"},{"id":"R4","met":"yes","where":"Introduction"},{"id":"R5","met":"partial","where":"Introduction"},{"id":"R6","met":"yes","where":"Launch"},{"id":"R7","met":"yes","where":"Orbit"},{"id":"R8","met":"partial","where":"Orbit; Mission Duration & EOL"},{"id":"R9","met":"yes","where":"Ground Control & Communications"},{"id":"R10","met":"partial","where":"Mission Duration & EOL"},{"id":"R11","met":"partial","where":"Orbit; Ground Control & Communications"},{"id":"R12","met":"partial","where":"Orbit; Ground Control & Communications"},{"id":"R13","met":"yes","where":"Executive Summary; Contents; Bibliography"},{"id":"R14","met":"partial","where":"Orbit; Ground Control & Communications"},{"id":"R15","met":"no","where":"Bibliography"}],"technical_issues":[{"where":"Launch","issue":"Report states equatorial launch maximises rotational velocity; this applies to prograde orbits, not retrograde SSO, so Kourou choice is not justified."},{"where":"Orbit","issue":"Initial RAAN 27.33° is described as indicating launch facility location; RAAN is an inertial node angle, not a ground-site coordinate."},{"where":"Orbit","issue":"Text says very high altitude yields low resolution images which is also desirable; for fluid-lensing reef imaging low resolution is undesirable."},{"where":"Orbit","issue":"Assumes descending passes are constantly sunlit; SSO is continuously sunlit only for dawn-dusk LTAN, which is not specified."},{"where":"Orbit","issue":"No sensor FOV, ground sample distance, swath or data-rate calculation supports 600 km altitude or 250 km reef coverage."},{"where":"Ground Control & Communications","issue":"Selects Mt Pleasant radio observatory without showing compatible comms band, antenna gain, link budget or data rate; radio astronomy suitability unproven."},{"where":"Ground Control & Communications","issue":"Access table has access 6 start/stop spanning about a day but duration 365 s, and access 15 duplicates access 14; access results need checking."},{"where":"Mission Duration & EOL","issue":"EOL section says satellite has no thrusters, yet orbit section calls EOL an orbital manoeuvre; passive drag sail is not a manoeuvre."},{"where":"Mission Duration & EOL","issue":"Drag-sail deorbit from 600 km not sized or timed; no demonstration of 25-year rule or complete burn-up."},{"where":"Mission Duration & EOL","issue":"No orbit maintenance or drag analysis over five-year life; replacement plan is generic."},{"where":"Bibliography","issue":"Uses Wikipedia [6] as a source and lists bare URLs/malformed entries, contrary to referencing requirements."},{"where":"Orbit; Ground Control & Communications","issue":"Figures and tables lack figure/table numbers and full captions, so cross-referencing is incomplete."}],"overall":"A readable preliminary design with adequate introduction and STK access work, but main design lacks payload/swath/link/EOL calculations and contains conceptual errors; referencing and figure/table labelling are inadequate.","criteria":[{"id":"C1","level":"Accomplished","mark":7.0,"scale":10.0,"points":7.0,"max":10.0,"reasoning":"Introduction clearly states coral-reef monitoring need, one-spacecraft rationale and likely government/science users, with references. However operator/customer distinction is vague and citations are poor.","evidence":[{"where":"Introduction","quote":"Coral reefs, specifically the Great Barrier Reef, would immensely benefit from dedicated satellite monitoring."},{"where":"Introduction","quote":"Thus, only one satellite is required."},{"where":"Introduction","quote":"The proposed satellite would likely be used by governmental and science agencies (e.g. CSIRO, BOM) for research and/or education."}]},{"id":"C2","level":"Adequate","mark":5.0,"scale":10.0,"points":30.0,"max":60.0,"reasoning":"Covers launch, orbit, ground station and EOL with STK access evidence and orbital elements, but lacks payload/swath/link calculations and software files, and contains conceptual errors on RAAN, sunlight and equatorial launch.","evidence":[{"where":"Ground Control & Communications","quote":"The first fifteen access periods are listed above. An average contact duration of almost ten minutes was achieved"},{"where":"Launch","quote":"The proposed satellite will thus use the Vega E rocket as a launch vehicle – launched from the Guiana Space Centre Spaceport."}]},{"id":"C3","level":"Adequate","mark":5.0,"scale":10.0,"points":5.0,"max":10.0,"reasoning":"Conclusion summarises launch, orbit and ground station, but is generic, omits mission duration/EOL and specific orbital/contact values.","evidence":[{"where":"Conclusion","quote":"After analysis of each mission segment, a fully described satellite configuration has been constructed."},{"where":"Conclusion","quote":"A specific launch vehicle – the Vega E – has been selected although it has not been built yet."}]},{"id":"C4","level":"Adequate","mark":5.0,"scale":10.0,"points":10.0,"max":20.0,"reasoning":"Required sections and headings are present and readable, but figures/tables are unnumbered, bibliography uses bare URLs and Wikipedia, formatting and spelling are inconsistent.","evidence":[{"where":"Bibliography","quote":"[6] https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2021_in_spaceflight. [Online]."},{"where":"Image 1","quote":"STK 3D view of orbit: 12 hours apart"}]}]}
